.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-admin-catalog-chains - Every blockchain configured in this environment, the ones not in service included


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 admin catalog chains [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
List every blockchain this gateway is configured with, whole rows: 'active' says
whether it is in service, 'rpc_urls' are the endpoints the gateway itself calls.

.PP
\&'rail0 chains' lists only the chains a payment can be made on. A chain here and not
there is configured and not serving: switched off, or missing a token or a deployment
('rail0 admin catalog tokens', 'rail0 admin catalog contracts').

.PP
A keyed RPC endpoint is shown as its template, with a {RAIL0_RPC\fIKEY\fP} placeholder:
the key is in the gateway's environment, never in this answer.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for chains


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 admin catalog chains --pretty
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-admin-catalog(1)\fP
